  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts with a custom GLSL water surface and a sky dome. It passes typecheck. I couldn't run `vite build` or open it in a browser here, so the rendering is unchecked. A shader compile error would show up as `CUSTOM_SHADER_COMPILE_FAILED` and a skipped draw.\n\n- **Water:** a 240×240 subdivided grid, 240 units across. The vertex shader displaces it with four summed sine waves and computes the normal analytically. The fragment shader adds noise ripples, a Schlick fresnel mix against the reflected sky, and a sun glint, then fades into the horizon colour with distance.\n- **Sky:** a large inward-facing sphere with a second custom material. It draws a horizon-to-zenith gradient with a sun glow and disc. The water samples the same sky function, so it reflects the sky.\n- **Animation:** the water reads a `waveClock` uniform that `onFrame` updates every frame. The camera sits 3.5 units above the water and looks slightly above the horizon.\n- **Colour:** both shaders write linear light and the app sets `output: { toneMapping: \"none\" }`, following the docs' custom-shader colour rules.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set two animation frames after `app.ready()` resolves. I avoided top-level `await` because the project has no Vite config, so the default build target may reject it.\n\n",
